Here is a Java program that finds the median of a given array using the divide and conquer algorithm:

import java.util.Scanner;

public class MedianFinder {

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

    System.out.print("Enter the size of the array: ");
    int n = scanner.nextInt();

    int[] arr = new int[n];

    for(int i=0; i<n; i++) {
      System.out.print("Enter the element at index " + I + ": ");
      arr[i] = scanner.nextInt();
    }

    double median = findMedian(arr, n);

    System.out.println("The median value of the array is: " + median);

    scanner.close();
  }

  public static double findMedian(int[] arr, int n) {
    if(n%2 == 0) {
      // if the array has an even number of elements
      int mid1 = n/2 - 1;
      int mid2 = n/2;
      double median = (double)(arr[mid1] + arr[mid2])/2;
      return median;
    }
    else {
      // if the array has an odd number of elements
      int mid = (n-1)/2;
      double median = (double)arr[mid];
      return median;
    }
  }
}

Explanation:
- The program takes the size of the array as user input and creates an empty array of that size.
- It then uses a for loop to take the elements of the array as user input.
- The program then calls the findMedian function, which takes the array and its size as parameters.
- In the findMedian function, if the array has an even number of elements, it calculates the average of the two middle elements and returns it as the median. If the array has an odd number of elements, it returns the middle element as the median.
- The main function then displays the median value of the array.

